Six engineers on one repository, each with an agent open, produce six branches
that never touch each other until they land. Up to that point everything runs at
whatever speed the machine underneath it runs. Then the branches meet, and a
protected branch takes one commit at a time, so the queue ends up being the one
piece of shared infrastructure every change passes through and the only serial
thing in a setup that is parallel everywhere else.

## Where a shared repo jams

[Xu, Subramanian and Karthik](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.04697v2) worked from the
AIDev-pop dataset: 33,596 agent-authored pull requests across 2,807
repositories. Counting exact temporal overlap, co-active pairs account for 79.4%
of every pull request an agent wrote. Widen the window to a week and it reaches
95.0%. They then replayed three-way merges on 747 co-active pairs, one per
repository, and counted textual conflicts: 41.7% for pairs written by different
agents, 19.8% for pairs written by the same one, with non-overlapping confidence
intervals.

Cross-agent pairs were 0.5% of the co-active pairs in that corpus. On a team
where each person runs their own agent, that rare case is the ordinary one, and
those overlapping pull requests are what arrives at the queue.

## Why a queue exists at all

Your suite runs on your branch. What lands is your branch combined with whatever
the base picked up while you were working, and git resolves that combination by
looking at text. Two changes can merge cleanly and still break each other:
somebody narrowed a type your new call site relies on, somebody renamed a fixture
you added a test against.

You answer that by serializing. The queue takes one pull request at a time and
builds the commit it would produce against the current base, so somebody learns
about the combination before it becomes the branch everybody pulls.

## Why the queue became the bottleneck

The usual way to find out is to run the suites on the integrated candidate in
the cloud, and to do it again for the next one, because merging the first
changed the base the second has to be built on.

[Bouzenia and Pradel](https://software-lab.org/publications/icse2024_workflows.pdf)
mined 1.3 million GitHub Actions runs across 952 repositories over 30 months for
ICSE 2024. They measured what one of those runs costs.

| Measured across 1.3M runs | Figure |
| --- | --- |
| Average job duration | 6.3 minutes |
| Share of resources on testing and building | 91.2% |
| Mean yearly spend, paid-tier repository | $504 |

Six pull requests behind a queue like that wait through six of those, one after
another, and a failure part-way sends its candidate back and rebuilds every
candidate behind it. Six agents working since breakfast can fill that line by
lunch, and the person at the back of it waits out every run in front of them
before their own starts.

## How this one moves

One lane per protected branch. `main` and `release` hash to different queue
keys, so they never share a turn. Inside a lane the scheduler is
`principal-round-robin-v1`: only a person's oldest eligible entry competes in a
round, and the person served least recently wins it, so ten pull requests from
one author cannot precede one pull request from somebody else.

The entry that wins the round gets a candidate pinned to it. GitHub computes
`refs/pull/N/merge` for every open pull request and rewrites it whenever either
side moves, so what the queue holds on to is the objects that ref pointed at:
the candidate commit, its two parents, and its tree.
`apps/control-plane/src/queue/candidate.ts` checks the parents before it will
hand any of that out.

```ts
const parents = input.candidate.parentShas;
if (parents.length !== 2) return { status: "blocked", reason: "CANDIDATE_PARENTS_UNEXPECTED" };
if (parents[0] !== pr.baseSha || parents[1] !== pr.headSha) {
  return { status: "blocked", reason: "CANDIDATE_PARENTS_UNEXPECTED" };
}
```

A first parent that is not the current base means GitHub simulated the merge
against a branch tip that has since moved, and if the second parent has drifted
off the head then it simulated different code. Seven states block a candidate
outright and one more says GitHub has not finished computing mergeability. Each
of the eight travels as its own code, because the queue acts on a conflict
differently from how it acts on an answer GitHub still owes it.

The profile in `thwip.yaml` decides what the queue will merge that candidate on.
Under `fast`, the evidence is the proof the developer signed on the pull request
head. `requiresIntegratedRun` returns false for `fast` and true for the other
three. The merge record it writes is a `reused-proof-merge`. Seven kinds of
record exist and that one is alone in claiming no run against the integration.

Under `fresh`, the queue signs an assignment naming the candidate tree, the
required suites and the policy behind them, and offers it to a device that
claimed the turn. That machine verifies the assignment against the published
service keys, fetches the candidate, runs the suites, and signs a receipt over
the tree it built. `requiresRemoteVerification` returns false for `fresh`, so
there is no fallback into Actions.

`audited` is `fresh` with every required suite proven a second time by GitHub's
own checks. `remote` is that second execution on its own. All three take a run
lease good for 1800 seconds; `fast` takes none.

A signature proves a run happened on a tree. The tree it covers is the branch
before integration, and GitHub writes a different commit to `main`. A queue
holds that commit. Everything a queue on its own knows about it comes from a run
the queue starts in the cloud and pays for.

In the same system, the queue fixes the candidate before it hands the work out,
so there is one tree to run and one tree to sign. A merge then lands in seconds
and the evidence covers the commit that landed, because the signature is what
lets the queue take a result from a machine nobody there owns or watched.

On this repository, running `fast`, the lane held these:

| The lane, 9 merges | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Turn offered to merge confirmed, median | 14.0s |
| Fastest | 11.6s |
| Slowest | 19.2s |
| Merge confirmed to signed record | 3.1s to 4.7s |

The write itself is GitHub's. Across 111 merge operations the call took 3.3
seconds at the median, 2.2 at the fastest and 4.3 at p90.

The same repository's pinned verifier workflow is what `remote` and `audited`
spend on every merge. It runs in Actions:

| The cloud run, 18 successful jobs | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Median wall clock | 73s |
| Fastest | 64s |
| Slowest | 85s |

Between 31 July and 4 August 2026 the queue recorded 138 movements of this
branch. Of the 96 the chain serves with signed bytes, 86 are reused-proof
merges, 5 are candidate-verified merges, 4 are external transitions and 1 is a
base correction.

## What an agent can see

An agent reads the queue over one authenticated call.
`GET /v1/queue/:repositoryId?ref=refs/heads/main` answers with every entry's
state, its blocking reason, its fairness ordinal, whether the scheduler will
consider it, and when its cooldown ends. That read is served from a D1
projection so a polling dashboard never wakes the queue object; `?live=1` reads
the object for an operator who cannot afford to be four seconds stale.
`/explain` adds a sentence per entry saying what is holding it.

The reasons are codes, from a closed union of 113 across five vocabularies.

| Vocabulary | Codes |
| --- | --- |
| Queue blocking reasons | 28 |
| Attempt outcomes | 19 |
| Proof verifier | 29 |
| Control plane, on the wire | 28 |
| Raised by the CLI about this machine | 15 |
| Distinct, after the six that appear twice | 113 |

Each of the 113 has exactly one sentence, in a `Record<ReasonCode, string>` that
the check, the CLI and the dashboard all read. Adding a code without writing its
sentence fails the build. The three surfaces used to keep their own copies and
the copies drifted, so one event read as "the source changed" in a terminal and
"push a fix" on a web page, which sent somebody to edit code that was fine.

A person gets the same facts as one comment on the pull request, edited in
place. It gives the position, the length of the line, the numbers of the pull
requests ahead, and the reason sentence for whatever state the entry is in.
GitHub sends no notification for an edit, so the comment can follow the queue as
closely as it likes. The queue fingerprints
everything the comment says apart from the clock and writes nothing when the
fingerprint matches, so a tick where nothing moved costs no GitHub calls.

Every merge leaves a signed record, and `GET /v1/queue/records/:digest` returns
the exact bytes that were signed. A `reused-proof-merge` carries the proof's
digest, the policy digest, the pipeline digest, the required-suites digest and
the check run that accepted the proof. A `candidate-verified-merge` carries the
verifier's result instead. They are separate kinds in the schema and each
refuses the other's fields, so months later you can still tell which one a
merge was.

## What it refuses

When the queue assembles the evidence for a reuse merge, anything it cannot read
stops the merge and gets named, so an absent value has no route through this
code that ends in a signature.

```ts
if (policy === null) return { missing: "the repository has no active policy" };
if (policy.pipelineDigest === null) return { missing: "the active policy names no pipeline" };
if (proofCheckRunId === null) {
  return { missing: "no passing proof check was found for this head" };
}
```

Further down, a head whose proof binds a policy the merge cannot read stops
there too, and a proof that ran a different pipeline or skipped a required suite
comes back as `POLICY_CHANGED` with the suite named. A head commit carrying no
readable proof digest ends the walk with `WORK_RECEIPT_UNREADABLE`, because the
merge record would otherwise cite nothing. Each of these was once a placeholder
constant, and a record built on a placeholder reads as evidence while binding
nothing.

The vocabularies work the same way. A value the parser does not recognise
fails, and a fact nobody has read carries the word `"unread"`, so a reviewer can
see the difference between a check that passed and a check nobody made.

The published chain shows a limit right now. All 96 signatures on it verify
against the keys at `/.well-known/thwip-keys`, and the walk still prints
findings, because the chain route omits record 121, an external transition the
index holds. The record after it names a predecessor nobody was served, and the
walk reports a break there. Those findings are correct, and the chain stays
broken until that record is served.

## What this does not buy

A signed receipt from a device says a suite passed on a tree. Whether the change
is a good idea is a question somebody still has to answer by reading the code.
Under `fast` an attacker holding an enrolled key can sign a
proof over a tree whose suites never passed, and the queue will merge it;
`audited` and `remote` answer that by running the suites in Actions and paying
for the run.

The five candidate-verified merges above came from the pinned verifier in
Actions. This branch runs `fast`, so the `fresh` numbers are from
`recallnet/thwip-test`: seven merges under that profile, five of them on 4
August 2026. On each of the five the queue signed an assignment over the
candidate tree, naming `lint` and `test` as the suites and policy v4 as the
authority for them. Before it fetched the candidate the device checked that
signature against the published keys. The control plane accepted the receipt a
median 7.4 seconds after the queue signed, and the `verified-merge` record
followed at 28.0 seconds, 26.6 at the fastest and 33.2 at the slowest. The chain
there reads 7 links with 7 of 7 signatures checked and no findings.

## Sources and code

The monorepo is [public](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip) and it merges
through this queue. The scheduler is
[`packages/queue-domain`](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/tree/main/packages/queue-domain),
the record schema is
[`packages/queue-protocol`](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/tree/main/packages/queue-protocol),
and both ship with their tests. Short forms for agents: [llms.txt](/llms.txt),
[faq.md](/faq.md).

